tvOS Browser
=============

![Alt text](/screen01.png?raw=true "tvOS Browser on AppleTV")

![Alt text](/screen02.png?raw=true "tvOS Browser Tab Switcher")

![Alt text](/screen03.png?raw=true "tvOS Browser Menu")


***tvOS Browser*** is a webbrowser for Apple TV devices running tvOS. It's using private API's, Apple does normally not allow to use this in App Store distributed apps. The latest version has some significant changes:
- It's now using WKWebView instead of UIWebView, which results in much improved performance and better website rendering.
- Added support for browser Tabs
- New modern Liquid Glass UI
- Added (experimental) full screen video player, which you can enable in the Menu

This repository is a fork of [jvanakker/tvOSBrowser](https://github.com/jvanakker/tvOSBrowser) with the bug fixes and improvements listed below.

This code is provided as is with no warrenty or liability. Use at your own risk and do ***not*** distribute builds of this project on the App Store.


What's New in This Fork
=============

Improvements
-------------
- **Ad blocker (on by default).** Blocks requests to ~110 ad, pop-under and tracker networks with WebKit content rules, plus third-party scripts on the throwaway domain endings rotating ad networks use (.cfd, .cyou, .qpon, .sbs, .icu...). Removes injected overlay ads (fake "payment"/system alerts, in-page push) that stack at the browser's top layer, even from unknown domains, while normal site dialogs stay. Hides leftover ad slots, refuses pop-ups a page opens by script/form/iframe to another site (a "Blocked a pop-up" message appears), and blocks page-initiated redirects to ad domains. Turn it off or on from the side menu under Privacy.
- **Apple TV–style remote navigation.** In navigation mode (double-press the touch area to switch from cursor mode), click the edges of the remote's touch surface to move a highlight between links, buttons and other clickable items, just like native tvOS apps. The site's own focus style is shown when it has one (an outline, focus shadow or border); otherwise a bright blue ring marks the selected item. Moves typically take 25-70 ms: the clickable elements are cached and only re-scanned when the page changes, items hidden under overlays and tiny targets (such as carousel dots) are skipped, auto-hidden video player controls stay reachable, and Select clicks a point where the item is actually visible. Left/right stay on the current row, up/down move between rows, the page scrolls to keep the highlight in view (or scrolls by itself when nothing is further that way), Up at the top of the page moves into the top bar, and Select opens the highlighted item. Swiping still scrolls freely.
- **Embedded players (iframes) are usable.** Many streaming and sports sites show a player embedded from another site. The browser's clicks, hover and navigation highlight now reach inside it: click its buttons (unmute, play, fullscreen, servers) in cursor mode, highlight the player in navigation mode and press Select, and press Play/Pause to control it. Its Fullscreen button works too, and Menu exits as usual. A muted stream (players start muted) is unmuted by the first Play/Pause press. Only the app can send these clicks, and an embedded frame can only go fullscreen right after you clicked into it, so ad frames cannot take over the screen.
- **Native Home page.** Home and new tabs open a built-in start page instead of a web URL: the browser wordmark, a search/address field, a grid of **Pinned** sites (your Favorites, shown with each site's own icon or a coloured monogram) and the last 10 **Recently Closed** tabs. Press and hold Select on a tile for Open, Open in New Tab, Pin, Remove and Clear options. The page is fully driven by the tvOS focus engine, and you can swipe up from it into the top bar.
- **Redesigned top bar.** SF Symbol buttons grouped as navigation (back, forward, reload), a centred address field (lock icon for https, clean `site.com/path` display) and actions (home, tabs, hide bar, menu). Focus uses the standard tvOS white lift instead of a blue glow, back/forward are dimmed when unavailable, the tabs button shows how many tabs are open, and the home icon is filled while on Home.
- **Page loading indicator.** The address field fills left to right with WebKit's real load progress, together with a spinner. When the top bar is hidden, a thin progress line runs across the top of the screen instead.
- **Redesigned Tabs page.** Full screen over a blurred backdrop, with a header showing how many tabs are open and on-screen remote hints. Cards have proper thumbnails, site icons, a "Current" badge, a white focus ring and a "Close" hint, and the New Tab card shows when the 10-tab limit is reached.
- **Persistent site data.** localStorage, IndexedDB, cookies and caches now survive app restarts. tvOS does not let apps write to WebKit's default location, so site data is kept in the app's Caches folder (tvOS may clear it if the device runs low on space).
- **Fullscreen for web video.** tvOS WebKit has no fullscreen API and its native video fullscreen shows a black screen, so the browser provides the standard fullscreen API itself: a site's Fullscreen button stretches its player over the whole screen and hides the top bar, with the site's own controls, subtitles and menus still working. Press Menu to exit.
- **Website subtitles in the full screen player.** The subtitles a site loads for its player (the SRT/WebVTT file it downloads when you pick a language, or the captions showing in a standard player) are carried into the native full screen player and shown in sync, with a Subtitles menu (Off / Website subtitles) in its transport bar. Seek-preview thumbnail tracks are ignored.
- **Full screen player: Quality menu and position sync.** The native full screen player (enable it in the side menu) has a Quality menu in its transport bar (swipe down while playing) listing the qualities the stream really offers (Auto, 4K, 1080p, 720p...) and the one playing now. A new choice applies immediately (the stream restarts from the same position) and is remembered as your default. Streams that come in a single quality say so; choose a quality or server in the site's own player instead. It starts where the page's video was and hands the position back when closed. Note that many 4K streams are HDR-only (marked HDR in the menu); Apple TV only picks them when the TV is in an HDR mode, so enable Settings > Video and Audio > Match Content (Dynamic Range) or set the format to 4K HDR.
- **Hover support and video-friendly remote.** Moving the cursor (or just touching the surface) sends mouse-move events to the page, so hover UI such as auto-hiding video player controls appears as it would with a mouse; in navigation mode, arrow presses, touches and Select do the same. On a page with a large video, Play/Pause pauses and resumes it (other pages still open the Quick Menu).
- **Media diagnostics mode** for debugging sites on a real Apple TV (see below).

Bug fixes
-------------
- **Videos only playing in the full screen player.** tvOS WebKit refuses to start video with sound unless a click is happening at that exact moment, and without MediaSource players hand their stream to the video only after calling play() on the still-empty element, which the new source cancels. Videos stayed paused at 0:00 on the page. Pages may now start playback themselves, and a video the page asked to play resumes once its new source is ready (unless it was paused since).
- **Selecting player controls opened the full screen player.** In navigation mode the highlight wandered into a player's invisible menus (faded out or click-through), and Select clicked at a point that landed on the video picture. Hidden items are skipped, and Select now presses the highlighted element itself.
- **Quality changes in the full screen player doing nothing.** AVPlayer keeps about a minute of video buffered and only applied a new quality to later downloads. The change now applies immediately.
- **Full screen player failing with "Cannot Open".** HLS streams served from addresses without `.m3u8` (and some MP4 hosts) went through a loader that cannot play them. The player now retries the stream directly.
- **Sports streams covered by ads.** On sports streaming sites a pop-under network injected a full-screen ad layer over the player, and a transparent ad link sat over the video so the first click opened an ad tab. Those networks (including one that rotates domain names, matched by its script path) are now blocked, and links to blocked ad hosts are hidden in every frame.
- **Links that open a new window doing nothing at the tab limit.** With all tabs in use, links such as a site's "Watch" buttons (which open in a new window) showed a "Maximum Tabs Reached" alert and loaded nothing. They now open in the current tab.
- **Subtitles disappearing in fullscreen.** The browser offered iOS-style video fullscreen (`webkitEnterFullscreen`), so players took their iOS route, which switches off their own subtitles in favour of native captions (empty on tvOS). That API is now hidden, so players use standard fullscreen on their own container and keep their subtitles and controls. The fullscreen container also no longer lands on invisible `display: contents` wrappers.
- **Navigation highlight left behind.** After Select opens an item the highlight is removed (the next arrow press continues from that spot on the same page), and it resets when the page changes, including in-page route changes on single-page sites.
- **Full screen player controls could not be brought back.** An old input hack swallowed Select, Play/Pause, left/right and swipes while the native player was open (turning Select into play/pause), so once its controls hid, clicking could not show them again. The player now gets the remote directly: click to show the controls, Play/Pause to toggle, left/right to skip, swipe to scrub, Menu to close.
- **Back button leaving the app.** Every Back/Menu press reached tvOS, which leaves the app before the browser could go back. Back now steps back through the tab's history, closes a tab opened from a link and returns to the tab it came from, leaves Home, fullscreen, menus and the top bar, and only goes to the Apple TV Home screen when there is nothing left to go back to (replacing the old "Exit App?" prompt).
- **Launch failure on newer SDKs.** Adopted the UIKit scene lifecycle ("UIScene life cycle is required for apps built with this SDK").
- **App freezing when moving the cursor.** The link-hover check ran synchronous JavaScript inside every touch event, which nested UIKit events until the app was killed ("sendEvent: nesting level exceeded"). It now runs after each event, one check at a time, and JavaScript waits time out instead of hanging.
- **Black pages after switching to scroll mode.** WebKit's page process crashes are now detected: the page reloads automatically and returns to its scroll position, and a "This page stopped responding" screen appears if it crashes again within 30 seconds. Scroll mode now uses only the app's own scrolling instead of scrolling every page twice.
- **Only trailers playing on some sites.** With the full screen player enabled, Select on a site's Play button grabbed the muted background trailer playing behind it. Decorative background videos are now ignored, and buttons or links get a normal click.
- **Black full screen player for HLS streams.** Streams that need the page's headers went through a custom loader that cannot serve HLS segments (CoreMedia error -12881). HLS now loads directly with the headers applied by AVFoundation.
- **Player controls opening the full screen player.** With the full screen player enabled, a player's own buttons (settings, subtitles, servers...) were treated as the video, and in navigation mode the check even looked at the hidden cursor instead of the highlighted button. Page controls now always receive the click; only Select on the video picture itself opens the full screen player.
- **Movies not loading (HLS streams).** tvOS WebKit has no MediaSource, so players such as hls.js hand the `.m3u8` straight to `<video>`, and those requests carry no `Origin` header, which many video hosts reject. Cross-site HLS video is now loaded in CORS mode so the header is sent, and the full screen player sends the page's Origin/Referer too.
- **Unwanted clicks after using the top bar or Home.** Selecting a top bar button or a Home tile no longer also clicks at the cursor position on the page underneath.
- **Site storage silently lost** on every app restart (see Persistent site data above).


How to Install tvOS Browser
=============

There is ***no*** ready to use binary available on this repository. You need to install Xcode and build/run the project yourself.
To install this app, connect your Apple TV to your macOS computer via WiFi (or USB on older devices). Open this project in Xcode, select your own development team and a unique bundle identifier under Signing & Capabilities, and install to your Apple TV.
Connecting to your Apple TV wirelessly: http://www.redmondpie.com/how-to-wirelessly-connect-apple-tv-4k-to-xcode-on-mac/.

How to Use tvOSBrowser
=============

Quick start:
- Double press on the center of the touch area of the Apple TV Remote to switch between cursor & navigation mode (a short message confirms the mode).
- Press the touch area while in cursor mode to click.
- In navigation mode, click the edges of the touch surface (up/down/left/right) to move between links and buttons, press Select to open the highlighted one, and swipe to scroll.
- Swipe up (or press up) to move into the top bar; on the Home page, simply move focus up from the tiles.
- Press Back (Menu) to go back to the previous page in the tab. On the first page of a tab opened from a link, Back closes it and returns to the tab it came from; on the Home page it returns to the page you came from. When there is nothing left to go back to, Back goes to the Apple TV Home screen.
- Single tap the Play/Pause button to pause/resume the video on video pages, or elsewhere to show the Quick Menu, with: input URLs, search Google, reload the page, or go forward.
- Double tap the Play/Pause button to: display the full Side Menu, with: Favorites, History, open the Home page, change user agent, clear cache, and clear cookies and more.
- On the Home page, press and hold Select on a pinned site or recently closed tab for more options.
- On the Tabs page, press Play/Pause to close the focused tab.

The Side menu lets you:
- Go to the Home page
- Manage Favorites (shown as Pinned sites on the Home page)
- Manage History
- Show Tabs
- Open New Tab
- Hide/Show top Navigation bar
- Set page Scaling options
- Increase/decrease Font size
- Enable/disable Full Screen player
- Turn the Ad Blocker on/off
- Switch Desktop/Mobile User Agent
- Show some video diagnostics
- Clear cache
- Clear cookies
- Show the usage guide

Media Diagnostics
=============

To debug why a site misbehaves on a real Apple TV, launch the app with the argument `-BrowserMediaDiagnostics YES` (in Xcode: Edit Scheme > Run > Arguments). Lines prefixed with `[BDIAG]` are printed to the console with the page's media capabilities (MediaSource, native HLS, WebAssembly, JavaScript speed), video element state and events, network requests, script errors and a storage persistence check. Add `-savedURLtoReopen <url>` to open a page at launch, and `-BrowserMediaDiagnosticsAutoPlay YES` to try starting the page's video automatically. The diagnostics also check the ad blocker (a known ad script should be blocked while a control script loads).

`-BrowserPlayerControlsDiagnostics YES -BrowserPlayerQualityDiagnostics YES` exercises a video page's player (settings, quality, fullscreen, Menu exit; add `-BrowserPlayerTestMode native` to test the full screen player's quality instead) and prints `[BQUAL]` lines.

`-BrowserBackDiagnostics YES` checks what the Back button does on a tab's first page, after navigating, and in a tab opened from a page, printing `[BBACK]` lines.

`-BrowserNavBenchmark YES` times 12 rapid arrow presses on the first page and prints `[BBENCH]` lines with the average and worst time.

`-BrowserFrameDiagnostics YES` presses Play/Pause for a player embedded in an iframe (add `-BrowserFrameClickPoint x,y` to click a point and test fullscreen), printing `[BFRAME]` lines; `-BrowserClickSelector '<css>'` clicks an element on the first page and reports the resulting tabs (`[BCLICK]`). With `-BrowserMediaDiagnostics YES`, every request the ad blocker blocks is printed, and iframes report their video state.

`-BrowserNavDiagnostics YES` runs a scripted sequence of remote arrow presses, a Select and a pop-up test on the first page that loads, printing `[BNAV]` lines with the element highlighted after each press.
